\title{Functional State & Asynchronous Portfolios}

\subtree{
  \title{Pure State Transitions & Epoch-Stamped Regional Ledgers}

  \p{
    Optimization state is an immutable tuple in the product space
  }

  ##{
    S \eqdef \mathcal{K} \times \mathcal{D} \times \prod_{r=1}^R T_r \times \mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{M}),
  }

  \p{
    joining a splittable 128-bit PRNG key #{k \in \mathcal{K}} (Claessen & Pałka [[CP13]](https://doi.org/10.1145/2503778.2503784), Steele, Lea, & Flood [[SLF14]](https://doi.org/10.1145/2660193.2660195)), global observation history #{\mathcal{D}}, #{R} concurrent local trust-region ledgers #{\{T_r(x_{0,r}, \ell_r, g_r)\}_{r=1}^R} (Eriksson et al. [[EPG+19]](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.01739), Daulton et al. [[DEB+22]](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v180/daulton22a.html)), and the compiled domain chart specification #{\mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{M})}. Multi-objective portfolios (#{M \ge 2}) draw per-region simplex weights to scalarize normalized objectives [[DEB+22]](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v180/daulton22a.html), while arm allocation across regions balances normalized incumbent value against completed plus in-flight evaluations.
  }

  \p{
    Asynchronous evaluation decouples global surrogate updates from regional side-length adaptation via epoch-stamped tickets #{\mathrm{AskId} = (r, g, a)}, recording originating region #{r}, its generation epoch #{g = g_r} at dispatch, and arm slot #{a}. Out-of-order completions via [baux.complete](docs.html#baux.complete) always append observations to #{\mathcal{D}}, whereas trust-region expansion/contraction on #{T_r} applies if and only if #{g = g_r}—preventing delayed evaluations from a pre-restart epoch from perturbing a freshly re-centered basin—and [baux.cancel](docs.html#baux.cancel) releases in-flight slot reservations without altering #{\mathcal{D}}. Cross-region coordination is governed by incumbent adoption: \code{sharing="origin_only"} restricts #{x_{0,r}} updates to region-#{r} proposals, whereas \code{sharing="ellipsoid_ball"} adopts any superior global observation falling inside the Euclidean ball circumscribing #{T_r}'s solver-coordinate box around #{x_{0,r}}, propagating local gains across overlapping basins while preserving distinct modes.
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{Splittable Rollouts & Canonical Snapshot Commutativity}

  \p{
    Because appending observations alters neighbor sets and regional incumbents, [baux.ask](docs.html#baux.ask) and [baux.tell](docs.html#baux.tell) form an order-dependent Mealy transition system rather than commuting updates. Reproducibility instead rests on pure functional folding ([baux.scan](docs.html#baux.scan)), pre-split parallel branch keys #{k \mapsto (k_1, \dots, k_B)} ([baux.pmap](docs.html#baux.pmap)) that render parallel rollouts invariant to worker scheduling, and lossless binary checkpointing. For a template state #{s_0 \in S} fixing #{\mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{M})} and any pure step transition #{\Phi_f : S \to S}, canonical serialization [baux.freeze](docs.html#baux.freeze) #{: S \to \mathcal{B}} and deserialization [baux.thaw](docs.html#baux.thaw) #{: \mathcal{B} \to S} commute with rollout execution #{\widehat{\Phi}_f \eqdef \mathrm{freeze} \circ \Phi_f \circ \mathrm{thaw}_{s_0}}:
  }

  ##{
    \begin{CD}
      S @>{\Phi_f}>> S \\
      @V{\mathrm{freeze}}VV @AA{\mathrm{thaw}_{s_0}}A \\
      \mathcal{B} @>>{\widehat{\Phi}_f}> \mathcal{B}
    \end{CD}
  }

  \p{
    The example below dispatches overlapping regional batches, completes and cancels tickets out of order via \code{batch.ids}, verifies canonical [baux.freeze](docs.html#baux.freeze)/[baux.thaw](docs.html#baux.thaw) idempotence and [baux.digest](docs.html#baux.digest) parity, and confirms bit-identical parallel [baux.pmap](docs.html#baux.pmap) rollout digests across restored snapshots:
  }

  \pre{
import numpy as np
import baux

bounds = np.tile([-3.0, 3.0], (8, 1))
weights = np.geomspace(1.0, 16.0, 8)


def anisotropic_loss(x):
    return -np.sum(weights * (x - 0.35) ** 2 + 0.1 * np.cos(3.0 * x), axis=1)


pipe = (
    baux.lhd(8)
    | baux.enn(k=6)
    | baux.thompson()
    | baux.regions(count=4, sharing="ellipsoid_ball")
)
s0 = baux.init(bounds, pipe, key=baux.key(101))
s_warm, b_init = baux.ask(s0, num_arms=8)
s_warm = baux.complete(s_warm, b_init.ids, anisotropic_loss(b_init.points))

s_live, b1 = baux.ask(s_warm, num_arms=2)
s_live, b2 = baux.ask(s_live, num_arms=2)
s_live = baux.complete(s_live, [b2.ids[0]], anisotropic_loss(b2.points[:1]))
s_live = baux.cancel(s_live, [b2.ids[1]])
s_live = baux.complete(s_live, b1.ids, anisotropic_loss(b1.points))

blob = baux.freeze(s_live)
restored_a = baux.thaw(s0, blob)
restored_b = baux.thaw(s0, baux.freeze(restored_a))
assert baux.freeze(restored_a) == blob
assert baux.digest(restored_a) == baux.digest(restored_b)

rollouts_a = baux.pmap(
    restored_a,
    baux.key(77),
    branches=4,
    branch_fn=lambda i, st, k: baux.digest(
        baux.scan(st, k, steps=4, eval_fn=anisotropic_loss, num_arms=2)[0]
    ),
)
rollouts_b = baux.pmap(
    restored_b,
    baux.key(77),
    branches=4,
    branch_fn=lambda i, st, k: baux.digest(
        baux.scan(st, k, steps=4, eval_fn=anisotropic_loss, num_arms=2)[0]
    ),
)
assert rollouts_a == rollouts_b and len(set(rollouts_a)) == 4
  }
}
